    <section id="risks">
        <h2>Risks & Complications</h2>
        <p>Like any surgical procedure, hair transplant carries potential risks. Understanding these helps you make an informed decision and recognize warning signs:</p>

        <h3>Common Side Effects (Temporary)</h3>
        <div class="highlight-box">
            <h3>✅ Expected Side Effects (Normal Recovery)</h3>
            <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                <li><strong>Redness:</strong> Lasts 3-7 days post-procedure</li>
                <li><strong>Swelling:</strong> Typically peaks at day 3, resolves by day 7</li>
                <li><strong>Scabbing:</strong> Small crusts form around grafts, fall off within 1-2 weeks</li>
                <li><strong>Shock loss:</strong> Temporary shedding of transplanted hair at 2-6 weeks — new growth begins at month 3</li>
                <li><strong>Numbness:</strong> Donor area may feel numb for 1-3 months</li>
                <li><strong>Itching:</strong> Normal during healing, avoid scratching</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>Rare Complications (Seek Medical Attention)</h3>
        <div class="warning-box">
            <h3>⚠️ Warning Signs to Watch For</h3>
            <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
                <li><strong>Infection:</strong> Increased redness, warmth, pus, fever — occurs in less than 1% of cases</li>
                <li><strong>Folliculitis:</strong> Red bumps or pustules around grafts — treatable with antibiotics</li>
                <li><strong>Unnatural hair direction:</strong> Grafts placed at wrong angles — usually due to inexperienced technicians</li>
                <li><strong>Permanent numbness:</strong> If donor area numbness persists beyond 6 months</li>
                <li><strong>Scarring:</strong> Visible scars from FUT or poor extraction technique</li>
                <li><strong>Graft failure:</strong> Less than 85% survival — may require touch-up procedure</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

